This feature is the opposite of [sequence extraction](/docs/gui-usage/sequence-extract/) feature. It removes sequences based on a list of IDs or a regular expression. It is faster than sequence extraction if you remove less than a half of the sequences.

## Steps

1. Select `Sequences` in the navigation bar {% ref id="A" /%}.
2. Open `Remove sequences` from the tool picker {% ref id="B" /%}: the tool list on wide windows, or the tool name at the top of the page on phones.
3. Add the input files {% ref id="C" /%} with `Browse files`, `Browse folder`, or `Open archive`. SEGUI searches a folder and its subfolders for files that match the tool's input format. See [file input](/docs/gui-usage/intro/#file-input) for mobile options.
4. Select the option to remove sequences based on a list of IDs or a regular expression.
5. Add the list of IDs or regular expression.
6. Select `Browse` under `Select output directory` to choose the output folder. On phones and tablets, enter a `Directory name` instead; SEGUI saves it in the app's own folder.
7. Select the output sequence format.
8. Select the run button {% ref id="D" /%}, labeled `Remove`, to start the task.

## Removal Options

### Remove by ID

The IDs should be separated by semicolon (`;`). For example, `seq1;seq2;seq3`.

### Remove by Regular Expression

SEGUI uses Rust [regex](https://docs.rs/regex/latest/regex/). The regular expression should be a valid regex pattern. For example, `seq[0-9]+` will remove all sequences with IDs like `seq1`, `seq2`, `seq3`, and so on.
